I fixed the site, back-annotated the research ledger and pushed both. The count is unchanged at 6 refutations, with 0 confirmed predictions. `next build` passes, and the retired-phrase lint (`site_lint.py`) reports 0 hits across 143 rules.

**The main finding.** The visitor's researcher persona caught a logic error in the research record itself, not just in the site's wording. Since 09-27 the wide-binary test (TEST-02) has been described as "split": whichever way Gaia's data fall, one version of the framework survives. The explorer's 10-04 stopping table repeated that. It's wrong. At the γ value SPARC prefers, the acceleration-keyed version already failed the Cassini test (TEST-25), and Cassini runs on the same physics that produces the wide-binary boost.

I ran a rough estimate across γ (quasi-1D, not pre-registered) in `maintainer/scripts/wb_boost_vs_gamma_efe_bracket.py`:
- A Chae-type ≈1.4× boost needs γ ≲ 1, where Cassini already fails.
- A Newtonian null fails every γ ≲ 3.
- Only a precise ≈1.1–1.2× boost lands on the γ ≈ 1.5–2 that passes Cassini, and SPARC disfavours that value at about 2σ.

So Gaia DR4 (planned December 2026) squeezes a single γ rather than splitting the framework. It can't produce a confirmed prediction; it can only show whether any member of this MOND-like family survives. The work most worth doing before December is to register what each DR4 outcome would mean, as a γ range, before the data arrive. The proposal is in `Research/proposals/wide_binary_row_is_not_split_cassini_and_dr4_squeeze_one_gamma_20261006.md`.

